# entrypoint.sh accepts either variable. It only ever reads them when a runner registers from
# scratch (no .runner in its volume) — restarts, recreates and rebuilds need neither.
#
# ACCESS_TOKEN — preferred. A PAT, exchanged for a fresh registration token on every start, so
# a runner whose volume was lost re-registers unattended. Classic scope `admin:org`, or
# fine-grained with organisation permission "Self-hosted runners: read and write". Nothing
# broader: any job on these runners can read it (the Docker socket is mounted, so a job can
# read these files through a container whatever their mode).
#
# Install it with the helper, which validates before writing and keeps the value out of
# argv and shell history:
#   pct exec 124 -- bash -c 'read -rs T && printf %s "$T" | /opt/gh-runner-docker/set-runner-pat.sh'
ACCESS_TOKEN=ghp_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# RUNNER_TOKEN — fallback, consulted only when ACCESS_TOKEN is unset. A registration token from
# Settings -> Actions -> Runners -> New self-hosted runner: single-use, valid about an hour.
# This is what the host held until now, which is why re-registration had stopped working.
#   gh api -X POST /orgs/eduinlight-org/actions/runners/registration-token --jq .token
# RUNNER_TOKEN=

# On the LXC these are /opt/gh-runner-docker/.env (service `runner`) and
# /opt/gh-runner-docker/runners.env (services `runner-2` .. `runner-4`), both chmod 600.
